In the weeks before Ontario's 2022 municipal elections, voter information letters with unique login credentials were being discarded, unopened, in recycling bins at rural post offices. Anyone who picked them up could have cast ballots on behalf of real voters.

That was the low-tech vulnerability. The high-tech one was worse. Researchers found that one-third of Ontario's online voting systems lacked protections against screen overlay attacks, where a hidden layer over the voting interface could redirect a voter's click to a different candidate without them knowing. On election night, up to one million ballots were potentially at risk.

These failures happened in a province that has been running online elections since 2003. A province where more than half of all municipalities now offer digital voting. A province that, until CAN/DGSI 111 was published in December 2024, had no national standard governing how any of it worked.

That standard, **CAN/DGSI 111-1:2024**, now exists. It is Canada's first national framework for online electoral voting, developed over years of academic research and built in direct response to documented failures. Every municipality planning an online election in 2026 should understand what it requires, what it still leaves open, and what systems are genuinely built to meet it.

---

## Two decades of digital elections with no common rules

Canada is one of the world's heaviest users of online voting at the municipal level. Ontario alone had **222 of its 444 municipalities** offering online voting in the 2022 elections, an increase from 180 municipalities in 2018. An estimated one million voters cast their ballots online in Ontario's 2018 elections alone. The 2026 cycle is expected to set a new record.

For most of that history, this happened with zero national standards. No federal or provincial requirements. No certification process. No shared security baseline. Each municipality procured independently from a market of competing vendors, with local clerks, not cryptographers, as the final accountability layer.

Prof. Nicole Goodman of Brock University, who co-developed the new national standard, summarized the situation plainly: "For a time, Canada was the only country in the world to use election technologies without regulations, even though Canada has one of the highest rates of use."

The fragmentation ran deep. Ontario's municipalities used six different vendors. Contracts were sometimes structured below competitive procurement thresholds, one municipality contracted voting services for under $10,000, bypassing competitive bidding entirely. Vendors were not subject to access-to-information law. No penetration test report had ever been publicly disclosed by any Ontario municipality in five years of academic research on the topic.

First Nations communities in Canada have faced their own parallel story. More than 50 First Nations have deployed online voting for band council elections and governance processes, served primarily through Indigenous technology companies like [OneFeather](https://www.onefeather.ca/), which now manages over 370,000 memberships across more than 289 nations. Research has found that 54% of Indigenous non-voters in the 2011 federal election said they would have voted if online voting had been available. Digital participation tools matter enormously in communities spread across vast distances. Yet the Indian Act and the First Nations Elections Act have historically required communities to have their own election code or self-government agreement before they can use any alternative voting method at all, a legal barrier that policy researchers have been calling on the federal government to remove.

---

## What researchers found: measuring Canada's online voting security

Before CAN/DGSI 111 was published, the same academics who would go on to co-develop it spent years auditing what was actually happening in Ontario. The findings, published in a 2023 peer-reviewed study by Essex, Goodman and colleagues (["Online Voting in Ontario Municipalities: A Standards-Based Review"](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-43756-4_4), _Electronic Voting_, Springer), are the most rigorous public audit of real-world online voting deployments available.

The benchmark was the Council of Europe's Standards for E-Voting, 141 requirements and guidelines that represent the most widely referenced international framework for digital election security.

**Ontario met 14% of applicable directives. That is 18 of 126.**

This is not a close call. The failures were systematic and documented across every major category.

**No city offered universal verifiability.** Not one municipality allowed any third party to independently verify that votes were counted correctly. The vendor tallied the votes and issued a result. There was no public proof, only the vendor's word.

**Ballot secrecy claims were false.** Simply Voting told municipalities that it was "impossible" for anyone to see how their members voted. A subsequent technical analysis found no application-layer cryptographic separation between a voter's choice and their authentication data. More than 50% of Ontario voters could be uniquely re-identified from their city and date of birth combined. Municipalities passed the vendor's secrecy claims on to the public unchanged.

**Scytl's "verifiability" feature violated ballot secrecy.** The system Scytl built to let voters verify their ballot showed them which candidate they had selected. That created a receipt. Any third party with a voter's credentials and a QR code could use that receipt to prove how someone voted, enabling precisely the vote-buying and coercion the feature was supposed to prevent.

**No coercion resistance existed anywhere.** None of Ontario's vendors provided technical protection against vote-buying or voter pressure in unsupervised remote environments.

**Transparency was essentially absent.** Not a single penetration test was ever made public. Vendor source code was proprietary. Audit logs were managed by the vendor, not the municipality. Two-thirds of the Council of Europe's transparency directives were unmet.

**Operational failures were common and undisclosed.** Thunder Bay voters received ballots for the wrong ward. Cambridge presented a ballot missing candidates. Dominion's systems caused service disruptions in more than 50 municipalities in 2018, requiring extended voting hours across the province.

Then came 2022. The [CBC investigation](https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/ontario-municipal-elections-online-voting-study-1.7644536) documented what the academic findings predicted: 70% of Ontario races were assessed at "high or extreme risk" of compromise. The PIN-in-the-recycling-bin vulnerability was real and documented. Scytl was patched mid-election after researchers alerted them. Neuvote went unpatched when polls closed.

This is the problem CAN/DGSI 111 was built to address.

---

## What CAN/DGSI 111-1:2024 actually requires

CAN/DGSI 111-1:2024, formally titled "Online Electoral Voting – Part 1: Implementation of Online Voting in Canadian Municipal Elections", was published as a National Standard of Canada by the Digital Governance Standards Institute (DGSI) in December 2024, and is [registered with the Standards Council of Canada](https://scc-ccn.ca/standardsdb/standards/4033474).

It was co-developed by Prof. Nicole Goodman (Brock University) and Prof. Aleksander Essex (Western University), with input from a technical committee of more than 100 experts across cybersecurity, political science, public policy and election administration.

**On voluntary vs. mandatory:** the standard is currently voluntary. No law requires municipalities to comply. There is no enforcement mechanism, no certification body and no provincial mandate. What the standard does provide is a procurement framework: designed to help municipal election administrators evaluate vendors, inform requests for proposals, and hold providers accountable to documented requirements. One jurisdiction has gone further. The Northwest Territories adapted CAN/DGSI 111 as the basis for their own territorial regulation and used it as the framework for the 2023 general election. The result: 96.4% of voters expressed high or moderate satisfaction. That is the pathway from voluntary to binding.

It is also worth noting that the original 2024 standard is currently being revised. A D1 draft was posted in December 2025 for public comment, with a March 2026 deadline. The 2026 Ontario municipal elections will run under the 2024 version while the updated standard is finalized. The standard is active and evolving at the same time.

### The core requirements

The standard specifies requirements across nine main areas:

**Vote secrecy and receipt-freeness.** No one, including system operators and election officials, can trace vote choices back to individual voters. The standard goes further: voters shall not be able to prove to anyone else how they voted. That second requirement is receipt-freeness - the property that prevents vote-buying and coercion structurally, not just procedurally - and it is a hard mandate in the standard, not a recommendation. The standard requires that privileged access to information be defined and that technical privacy guarantees be documented. "We say it's secret" is not a sufficient answer.

**Verifiable results.** The standard requires "independently verifiable evidence supporting the outcome of the election." More on what this means in practice below, there is significant variation in how "verifiable" can be interpreted.

**Voter authentication.** Documentation and processes for voter identity verification, addressing credential-based vulnerabilities like the 2022 PIN failures. Multi-factor approaches are implied by the requirement to protect authentication data.

**Data security and cryptographic standards.** Security thresholds for data in transit and storage across all devices and entities involved in the election. Two specific cryptographic mandates stand out: where votes are encrypted at the application layer on the client side, the standard requires threshold encryption for generating decryption keys, meaning no single party can decrypt results alone. The cryptographic module used must either undergo independent testing or be publicly auditable through open-source publication. Both are hard requirements that go well beyond what current Canadian vendors provide.

**Logic and accuracy testing.** Pre-election and post-election testing processes to verify system integrity before and after voting.

**Ballot design and accessibility.** Requirements for ballot clarity, usability and accessibility to ensure all voters can participate.

**Transparency and disclosure.** Information about how the system functions must be publicly available. Vendors cannot operate as black boxes.

**Procurement guidance.** Requirements for competitive and transparent procurement processes, directly targeting the below-threshold contracting documented in the academic audit.

**Accountability of the electoral management body.** The municipality must have meaningful control over the process, not just nominal oversight.

### The verifiability spectrum

The standard's verifiability requirement is its most important, and currently its most open, because "independently verifiable" can mean very different things in practice:

1. **Vendor-issued audit report**: A PDF from the vendor saying the results are correct. This is what most current Canadian vendors provide. It requires trusting the vendor.
2. **Administrative audit trail**: Logs and records that a third-party auditor can inspect under restricted access. Better, but still requires trusting the auditor.
3. **Cryptographic end-to-end verifiability**: Any voter can confirm their individual ballot was included in the count; any third party can verify the full result without trusting anyone. The proof is public and mathematically checkable.

The standard's top-level obligation here is a "should" - a strong recommendation, not a hard mandate. But the standard makes clear that if verifiable evidence is provided, the sub-requirements are binding: the evidence must be verifiable by a qualified independent observer, sufficient to convince observers of a correct result or detect an incorrect one, and accurate. The gap the standard leaves is in specifying _how_ verifiable. A vendor-issued PDF technically satisfies the letter; a cryptographic proof satisfies the intent.

The academic co-developers are explicit about what the evidence demands. Recommendation 5 in the 2023 paper calls directly for "cryptographic end-to-end verification" as the approach that would address the most unmet directives from the Council of Europe audit. That recommendation was written by the same researchers who co-authored the standard - it is the direction they are pointing, and future revisions are expected to make it a harder requirement.

On **coercion resistance**: the standard recommends (should) that systems support vote updating - the ability for a voter to silently change their choice before polls close - particularly for uncontrolled environments like voting from home. Where vote updating is not supported, providers must document alternative safeguards and residual risks. This is a recommendation, not a mandate. But paired with the hard receipt-freeness requirement, the intent is clear: the standard treats protection from vote-buying and coercion as a genuine design obligation, not an afterthought.

---

## Why this standard matters for election administrators

The standard is not primarily a security document. It is a governance tool that protects election administrators.

Before CAN/DGSI 111, a municipal clerk managing an online election had no common framework. Vendors made claims, "impossible to compromise," "bank-grade encryption," "fully auditable", and clerks had no tool to evaluate whether those claims were true. The research showed they often were not.

The standard changes this. It gives election administrators:

- A checklist of verifiable requirements to put to vendors in an RFP
- A common language that allows genuine comparison across vendors
- A documented basis for rejecting providers who cannot demonstrate compliance
- Legal and reputational protection when something goes wrong

The last point matters. An election administrator who can demonstrate they followed the national standard when procuring a voting system is in a far stronger position than one who relied on a vendor's marketing materials. The standard does not eliminate risk. It transfers accountability to where it belongs: the vendor's documented technical specifications, not the clerk's trust in a sales pitch.

Municipal clerks should not have to be cryptographers. The standard exists precisely so they do not have to be.

---

## How current vendors measure against the standard

No vendor has been formally certified against CAN/DGSI 111 - the certification infrastructure does not yet exist. What we can do is compare the standard's stated requirements against what the 2023 academic audit documented about the systems serving Canadian municipal elections at the time. The findings, from the Essex/Goodman peer-reviewed paper, cover the full Ontario market.

<!--kg-card-begin: html-->

| Requirement                                                                                        | Current Ontario vendors (Essex/Goodman 2023 audit)                                                    | DAVINCI / Vocdoni                                                 |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Universal verifiability** (any 3rd party can verify the full result)                             | Not provided by any vendor audited                                                                    | Full cryptographic E2E-V                                          |
| **Individual verifiability** (voter can confirm their ballot was counted)                          | Not provided; one vendor's attempt created a receipt that broke ballot secrecy                        | Full, without creating a receipt                                  |
| **Receipt-freeness** (SHALL: voter cannot prove their choice to a 3rd party)                       | Not provided by any vendor audited                                                                    | Yes, by design                                                    |
| **Ballot secrecy (technical)** (vote content structurally inaccessible to server)                  | Most vendors relied on transport-layer encryption only; server-side access to vote choices documented | Full - votes tallied without anyone decrypting individual ballots |
| **Threshold encryption** (SHALL where client-side encryption used: no single party decrypts alone) | Not implemented by vendors audited                                                                    | Yes, distributed key generation                                   |
| **No premature result access**                                                                     | Real-time server access to running tallies documented for at least one vendor                         | Full - votes stay encrypted until election close                  |
| **MITM / overlay attack protection**                                                               | Absent or incomplete in most systems audited; one-third of systems lacked overlay attack protection   | Yes                                                               |
| **Open-source cryptographic module** (SHALL: publicly auditable crypto code)                       | All vendors audited: proprietary, closed source                                                       | Yes, full codebase public                                         |
| **Penetration testing disclosed**                                                                  | No municipality publicly disclosed a penetration test in five years of research                       | Open source enables independent audit by anyone at any time       |
| **Coercion resistance / vote updating** (SHOULD: voter can silently change their vote)             | Not provided by any vendor audited                                                                    | Yes, vote overwriting by design                                   |
| **Transparency of procedures**                                                                     | Not provided; vendor source code and audit logs proprietary                                           | Yes, public code and public audit trail                           |

<!--kg-card-end: html-->

_Vendor assessments are based on the_ [_Essex/Goodman 2023 peer-reviewed audit_](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-43756-4_4) _of Ontario's online voting market. Individual vendors may have updated their systems since 2022. DAVINCI assessments are based on the published protocol documentation._

The gap is not a matter of vendors performing poorly on the same architecture. It is a structural difference. Current Canadian vendors were built on administrative security models: control who can access the system, encrypt data in transit, produce audit logs. DAVINCI is built on cryptographic security: the guarantee does not depend on trusting anyone, because the proof is public and mathematically verifiable.

Those are different designs, not different performance levels on the same design. Meeting the standard's receipt-freeness mandate, threshold encryption requirement, open-source cryptographic module requirement, and recommended verifiability levels would require a fundamental architectural rebuild, not a configuration update.

---

## What genuinely secure, auditable online voting looks like

The standard's verifiability requirement opens the door to a concept the academic community has been working on for years: **end-to-end verifiable voting**.

In most current online voting systems, you cast your vote, the server records it, and the vendor produces a result you are expected to accept. You have no way to confirm your vote was counted. You have no way to confirm no votes were added or removed. You trust the vendor.

End-to-end verifiability changes this. In a properly designed system:

- Every voter receives a way to confirm their individual ballot was included in the count, without revealing what they voted for
- Any independent observer can verify the full result and confirm it is mathematically correct
- The verification does not require trusting anyone, because the proof is public and checkable

This is not a theoretical concept. It is technically implemented in systems today. [The Bellpuig referendum](/blog/referendum-bellpuig), the first fully digital referendum conducted under Spanish law, used it. The result was certified and independently verifiable by any participant.

The research co-authors of CAN/DGSI 111 made end-to-end verifiable voting their explicit Recommendation 5: "Make election results evidence-based... independent verification such as cryptographic end-to-end verification would address many unmet directives."

The standard points in this direction. The architecture that supports it needs to be in place before the mandate arrives.

