## How Vocdoni and DAVINCI meet the standard's requirements

Vocdoni has been deploying digital voting since 2018. In seven years, the company has run elections for political parties, professional bodies, cooperatives, municipalities and national organizations with hundreds of thousands of members. The DAVINCI voting protocol is Vocdoni's next-generation cryptographic engine that powers the platform, [the architecture that makes results independently verifiable](/blog/davinci-universal-voting-protocol) in a way that no current Canadian vendor provides. The platform's [academic validation](/blog/vocdoni-upc-partnership), developed in collaboration with researchers at the Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya, is directly relevant to an audience that has seen what happens when electoral technology lacks rigorous independent scrutiny.

Here is how DAVINCI maps to each CAN/DGSI 111 requirement area.

<!--kg-card-begin: html-->

| CAN/DGSI 111 requirement area                   | How DAVINCI meets it                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Status                                                   |
| ----------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Vote secrecy**                                | Votes are encrypted at the moment they are cast. Tallying happens without anyone ever decrypting individual ballots, not the municipality, not Vocdoni. Voter identity is structurally decoupled from vote content by design.                                                                                                | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Threshold encryption**                        | No single party holds the decryption key. Multiple independent keyholders must cooperate to tally votes - the standard's explicit cryptographic mandate.                                                                                                                                                                     | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **No premature result disclosure**              | Votes stay encrypted until after the election closes. Even Vocdoni cannot see a running tally. The result is published automatically when the election ends.                                                                                                                                                                 | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Independent verifiability**                   | Public cryptographic proof: any voter, any observer, any independent expert can verify the full result without involving Vocdoni. [The public election record at explorer.vote](https://explorer.vote) shows this in practice.                                                                                               | Full, exceeds standard's SHOULD with cryptographic E2E-V |
| **No duplicate voting**                         | Cryptographic nullifiers: a unique hash derived from voter credentials prevents the same voter from submitting more than one final vote, provably and without exposing voter identity.                                                                                                                                       | Full                                                     |
| **Voter authentication**                        | Flexible census system, supports any identity provider (email, SMS, government ID systems, SSO). Eligibility verified cryptographically against a voter list.                                                                                                                                                                | Full                                                     |
| **MITM and client-side attack protection**      | Standard HTTPS plus a cryptographic proof chain: any tampering with the ballot in transit produces an invalid proof that is automatically rejected.                                                                                                                                                                          | Full                                                     |
| **Ballot secrecy / receipt-freeness**           | Voters cannot produce a receipt showing their vote. Even if they wanted to prove how they voted to a third party, the re-encryption mechanism makes this structurally impossible.                                                                                                                                            | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Coercion resistance / vote updating**         | Voters can silently overwrite their vote at any time before the election closes. Even if coerced, the coercer cannot verify the final vote. The standard recommends (SHOULD) vote updating for uncontrolled environments; DAVINCI provides it as a hard architectural guarantee.                                             | Full, exceeds standard's SHOULD                          |
| **Logic and accuracy testing**                  | Open source code means any municipality or independent party can inspect the full codebase, run test elections, and verify results before going live.                                                                                                                                                                        | Full                                                     |
| **Open-source cryptographic module**            | The standard requires the cryptographic module to be either independently tested or publicly auditable via open-source publication. DAVINCI's full cryptographic codebase is public.                                                                                                                                         | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Transparency and disclosure**                 | Code is public (GitHub). Election records are public (explorer.vote). Any party can inspect how the system works before, during and after an election.                                                                                                                                                                       | Full, exceeds standard                                   |
| **Ballot design and accessibility**             | Configurable ballot formats: single choice, multiple choice, ranked, weighted, quadratic. Device-agnostic: any browser, no app download required for voters.                                                                                                                                                                 | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Procurement transparency**                    | Public pricing at app.vocdoni.io/plans. Self-service. No sales call required for standard configurations.                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | Full                                                     |
| **Accountability of electoral management body** | Municipality or election administrator controls all election parameters, voter list, ballot, timing. Vocdoni cannot alter these after setup.                                                                                                                                                                                 | Full (SHALL)                                             |
| **Data localization**                           | The standard requires election data on Canadian servers. For decentralized systems, the requirement is satisfied where data outside Canada cannot reconstruct voter identity or alter results. DAVINCI's architecture satisfies this condition by design; Canadian-hosted nodes can be configured for municipal deployments. | Full via decentralized exception                         |

<!--kg-card-end: html-->

One requirement worth addressing directly: the standard mandates that all election data be stored on servers physically located in Canada. Vocdoni's infrastructure is EU-based. The standard includes an explicit provision for decentralized systems: the requirement is satisfied where election-critical components run on Canadian infrastructure, or where data stored outside Canada cannot be used to reconstruct voter identity or alter election results. DAVINCI's cryptographic design - where vote content is encrypted and individual votes are unlinkable to voters at the infrastructure level - satisfies the second condition. For Canadian municipal elections, Vocdoni would also configure election-critical nodes on Canadian-hosted infrastructure. This is an operational configuration, not an architectural barrier.

The current Canadian vendors do not fail CAN/DGSI 111 because of poor implementation. They fail because they were built for a different security model. Providing verifiable results and receipt-freeness requires architectural redesign, not a software update.

See [Vocdoni's voting architecture](https://vocdoni.io/technology) for a full technical explanation, or see [the difference between administrative and cryptographic security in practice](/blog/from-blockchain-voting-to-cryptographic-voting).

