## Where the standard goes from here

CAN/DGSI 111 is titled "Part 1" for a reason. It covers municipal elections. The path to provincial and federal coverage is being actively discussed by its co-developers.

The Northwest Territories built a regulatory framework for online voting informed by early drafts of CAN/DGSI 111 and applied it in the 2023 territorial election. The standard is the technical foundation; the law provides the enforcement. As the 2026 Ontario municipal elections run, the first major electoral cycle with the standard in place, the question of whether municipalities actually use it in RFPs will determine whether the voluntary framework has any practical effect.

Canada's experience here is genuinely unusual. Two decades of real-world online voting at scale, followed by systematic academic documentation of failures, followed by a standard developed by the researchers who found those failures. Few other jurisdictions have this combination. The Policy Options journal noted in 2022 that "as governments around the world seek to close gaps in regulatory frameworks... they may be looking to Canada." The Council of Europe's existing Recommendation CM/Rec(2017)5 predates many of the cryptographic advances in E2E-V and receipt-freeness; Canada's standard was built on more current research. Countries with emerging online voting adoption, across the Commonwealth, Latin America and Asia-Pacific, are watching what Canada does next.

The First Nations legal barrier is also moving. The call to amend the Indian Act and the First Nations Elections Act to allow communities genuine choice in their voting methods is gaining momentum. If those amendments come, the demand for digital voting infrastructure that meets or exceeds CAN/DGSI 111 requirements will grow sharply.

The standard is not a destination. It is the beginning of a conversation Canada should have had years ago.

---

## What this means for your next election

Whether you are a municipal clerk preparing for 2026, a governance officer for a professional association, or an election official evaluating vendors for the first time, CAN/DGSI 111 gives you something that did not exist before: a documented baseline for what secure digital elections require.

Use it as a procurement tool. Ask vendors to demonstrate compliance with each requirement, not claim it, demonstrate it. Ask specifically about independent result verification: can any third party check the result without trusting the vendor? Ask about ballot secrecy: is the protection technical and structural, or is it a vendor promise? Ask about receipt-freeness: can a voter prove to anyone how they voted? Ask to see the source code.

The standard raises the floor. The technology to exceed that floor already exists.

Vocdoni has been building it for seven years. If you are evaluating online voting software for an upcoming election, the difference between systems that meet the standard and systems that claim to is now clearly documented. [See how DAVINCI works](https://vocdoni.io/technology), or [run your first election free at app.vocdoni.io](https://vocdoni.io/app). No sales call required.
